[THOUGHT] The current evidence strongly supports malignant distal biliary obstruction, and pancreatic head adenocarcinoma is the most common cause of this clinical syndrome — especially given painless jaundice, Courvoisier sign, rapid weight loss, cholestatic LFTs, and imaging showing biliary dilation + gallbladder distension. However, the diagnosis remains *presumptive*: no histopathologic confirmation (e.g., EUS-FNA or ERCP biopsy), no cross-sectional imaging (MRI/MRCP or CT) to assess tumor size, vascular invasion, or metastases, and no CA 19-9 result to support malignancy in context. While clinical and imaging findings are highly suggestive, definitive diagnosis of pancreatic adenocarcinoma requires tissue confirmation per standard oncologic practice (per NCCN/ESMO guidelines). Since we are at Turn 12/16 — i.e., not yet at maximum turn limit — critical diagnostic data is still missing, and the diagnosis cannot yet be considered *confirmed* or *sufficiently established* for definitive management planning (e.g., surgical resection eligibility, staging, or neoadjuvant decisions). The differential still includes ampullary carcinoma and cholangiocarcinoma — which have different prognoses, resectability criteria, and therapeutic pathways — and these cannot be reliably distinguished without further imaging and tissue sampling.

[DECISION] INCOMPLETE

[FEEDBACK] Critical missing information:  
- MRI/MRCP results (to localize stricture, assess tumor morphology, vascular encasement, and metastatic disease);  
- CA 19-9 level;  
- Histopathologic confirmation (via EUS-FNA or ERCP brushings/biopsy);  
- Assessment of liver synthetic function (albumin, INR) and nutritional status (e.g., prealbumin, BMI trajectory) to guide procedural risk and treatment planning.

[THOUGHT] The Supervisor has identified four critical gaps:  
- MRI/MRCP (to precisely localize biliary stricture, assess tumor morphology, vascular involvement, and metastases);  
- CA 19-9 (a tumor marker highly sensitive for cholangiocarcinoma and pancreatic cancer, especially in cholestatic patients);  
- Histopathologic confirmation (required for definitive diagnosis — EUS-FNA or ERCP-based sampling);  
- Liver synthetic function (albumin, INR) and nutritional status (not yet obtained; albumin/INR were missing from prior LFT request).  

Of these, CA 19-9 is a simple, rapid, high-yield blood test that supports malignancy likelihood and helps interpret imaging. It is also prerequisite for interpreting MRCP findings (e.g., CA 19-9 >100 U/mL strongly favors malignancy over benign stricture). Albumin and INR are essential to assess surgical/ procedural candidacy and prognosis. Since only one test can be requested per turn, and albumin/INR were *already missing* from the initial LFT panel (and remain unreported), obtaining them now is both urgent and atomic.

[TEST] Serum albumin and INR.

